For `FILE_SEARCH`:
```c
case FILE_SEARCH:
        if (offset > nbytes)
                offset = CAST(uint32_t, nbytes);
        ms->search.s = RCAST(const char *, s) + offset;
        ms->search.s_len = nbytes - offset;
        ms->search.offset = offset;
        return 0;
```

This is safe - it clamps `offset` to `nbytes`.

For `FILE_REGEX`:
```c
case FILE_REGEX: {
        ...
        if (s == NULL || nbytes < offset) {
                ms->search.s_len = 0;
                ms->search.s = NULL;
                return 0;
        }
        ...
        buf = RCAST(const char *, s) + offset;
        end = last = RCAST(const char *, s) + bytecnt + offset;
        /* mget() guarantees buf <= last */
        for (lines = linecnt, b = buf; lines && b < end &&
             ((b = CAST(const char *,
                 memchr(c = b, '\n', CAST(size_t, (end - b)))))
             || (b = CAST(const char *,
                 memchr(c, '\r', CAST(size_t, (end - c))))));
             lines--, b++) {
                if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\r' && b[1] == '\n')
                        b++;
                if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\n')
                        b++;
                last = b;
        }
        if (lines)
                last = end;
```

Wait, there's a potential issue here. `bytecnt` is computed as:
```c
if (bytecnt == 0 || bytecnt > nbytes - offset)
        bytecnt = nbytes - offset;
if (bytecnt > ms->regex_max)
        bytecnt = ms->regex_max;
```

So `bytecnt` is at most `nbytes - offset`. Then `end = s + bytecnt + offset = s + nbytes` at most. So `end` is within bounds.

But in the loop:
```c
for (lines = linecnt, b = buf; lines && b < end &&
     ((b = CAST(const char *,
         memchr(c = b, '\n', CAST(size_t, (end - b)))))
     || (b = CAST(const char *,
         memchr(c, '\r', CAST(size_t, (end - c))))));
     lines--, b++) {
        if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\r' && b[1] == '\n')
                b++;
        if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\n')
                b++;
        last = b;
}
```

After the loop body, `b++` is executed. If `b == end - 1` (i.e., `b` points to the last byte), then `b++` makes `b == end`. Then the loop condition `b < end` fails, so the loop exits. But `last = b` was set to `end - 1` in the last iteration. Then `ms->search.s_len = last - buf` which is `end - 1 - buf`. This seems fine.

But wait, if `b == end` after `b++`, and the loop condition `b < end` fails, then `last` was set to `end - 1` in the last iteration. But what if `b` was `end - 1` and `b[0] == '\r'` and `b[1] == '\n'`? Then `b++` makes `b == end`, and then `if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\n')` - `b < end - 1` is false, so this is skipped. Then `last = b` which is `end`. Then `b++` makes `b == end + 1`. The loop condition `b < end` fails. So `last = end`. Then `ms->search.s_len = last - buf = end - buf = bytecnt`. This is fine.

